WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:02.920
- [Mark] All right, Ruby?

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:05.230
Ruby, where'd you grow up?

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.490
Where are you from originally?

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.000
- [Ruby] Leslie County.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.952
- [Mark] You grew up in Leslie
County here in Kentucky?

00:00:09.952 --> 00:00:11.060
- [Ruby] Yeah.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.812
- [Mark] How would you
describe your childhood?

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:15.640
- Well, I'd say I had the
most wonderful childhood

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.440
could of been, I stayed
right behind my dad's back.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.960
We raised five acres of farro beans,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.630
peddled them on the mining camps,

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.660
and then we'd take a load of
groceries back to White Oak

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.482
to the store from the
breaks along down here

00:00:31.482 --> 00:00:32.315
all the way to White Oak,

00:00:32.315 --> 00:00:35.130
we would sell everybody
groceries, stopped at her house

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.180
till we got in home,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.835
and we barely had anything from
the store when we got there.

00:00:38.835 --> 00:00:41.250
- [Mark] You were born in what year?

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.083
- 1934.

00:00:43.487 --> 00:00:45.303
I'll be 87 in April.

00:00:46.277 --> 00:00:51.250
- [Mark] 87, and so you
growing up here, you had

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.570
I assume you had no electricity.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.647
You had no running water.

00:00:53.647 --> 00:00:55.640
- Nobody didn't help that.

00:00:57.135 --> 00:00:59.650
And we got water out of a coal bank

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.780
and you took sacks and
went up to the coal bank

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.756
and drug your coal down
the hill to the house

00:01:03.756 --> 00:01:05.195
to burn in the night.

00:01:05.195 --> 00:01:06.028
- [Mark] To keep it warm?

00:01:06.028 --> 00:01:07.297
- Keep it warm.

00:01:07.297 --> 00:01:09.340
- [Mark] And for food you'd go where?

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.820
- No, we raised our
food. I still raise food.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.770
I've got three deep freezers full

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.200
and hogs to kill and everything.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.650
I don't hardly buy, I buy 50
pounds of pinto beans at a time

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.880
and a big stand of lard
like I growed up in.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.983
Big 25 bounds of sugar and salt,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.480
and you don't have to go to the store,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.410
but just like occasionally.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.780
- [Mark] Yeah, so you're, and you garden?

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.480
- Oh yeah, we raise everything.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.430
- [Mark] You grow everything?

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.750
You've done that your whole life.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.160
- Yeah. That's what I eat.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.667
That's the reason my health
is nothing wrong. I think.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.489
- [Mark] No, I see how
hard you're working.

00:01:45.489 --> 00:01:46.322
- Yeah.

00:01:46.322 --> 00:01:47.420
- [Mark] We found you here
at nine o'clock this morning

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.149
and you were coming back from,
from what were you doing?

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:50.982
(Ruby laughing)

00:01:50.982 --> 00:01:54.900
- I was getting ready, we got
put electric pole up today

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.953
to a trailer and start tearing one down.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.077
- [Mark] And you hunt for,
tell me what else you do?

00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:05.950
You stay busy all day long.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.220
- Yeah, I get up, well,
usually about five o'clock

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.860
and then I lay down maybe
seven or eight at night.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.956
I get it turned in like, and get in bed.

00:02:14.956 --> 00:02:18.150
- [Mark] But you spend your
days doing what kind of stuff?

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.393
- Well, mainly chasin' renters. (laughing)

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:25.663
- [Mark] Chasing what?

00:02:25.663 --> 00:02:27.780
- Renters. I rent properties.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.248
- [Mark] Oh, you rent properties,

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:30.147
so you're trying to get people--

00:02:30.147 --> 00:02:32.460
- Yeah, and the kids have
to have it for college.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.683
I had to give one boy this week $4,000.

00:02:36.251 --> 00:02:39.111
I went and paid tuition for him to get,

00:02:39.111 --> 00:02:43.660
drive big trucks, this week,

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.600
so that's what the rent goes for.

00:02:48.015 --> 00:02:49.840
- [Mark] And do you have
children of your own?

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.003
- Oh, yeah. I've got five generations,

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:56.343
and I've schooled all of them.
Sent them to college, so far.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.270
Right now, going and
getting them college hours

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.650
to drive a big tractor and trailer.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.020
He's getting out of the mines.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.057
He was driving five hours
a day to work and back.

00:03:08.057 --> 00:03:11.339
So I went and paid his
tuition was 4,000 this week,

00:03:11.339 --> 00:03:12.288
having that money.

00:03:12.288 --> 00:03:13.610
- [Mark] When you say you
have five generations,

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.710
you mean your children have had children

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:17.543
who have had children.

00:03:17.543 --> 00:03:19.760
- Yeah, that's what I'm putting this week.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.513
This one track right here,
this old Texico park.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.270
It goes into five generations

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.053
it can't be sold out of the family,

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:30.812
and this property all
belonged to my grandfather.

00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:31.645
- [Mark] How many acres?

00:03:31.645 --> 00:03:32.740
- To begin with.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:33.573
- [Mark] How many acres?

00:03:33.573 --> 00:03:34.990
- There is about 500 acres right here

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.274
in this thousand acre
patent, from 421 highway.

00:03:38.274 --> 00:03:39.930
- [Mark] And you're up
in a mountain top here.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:40.763
It's beautiful.

00:03:40.763 --> 00:03:42.360
- Yeah, on top this mountain.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.120
I had cancer in 1970 and they
closed my restaurant down.

00:03:48.175 --> 00:03:52.560
Or so they said, and I took that money

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.657
and sold out and come up here

00:03:53.657 --> 00:03:56.850
and my grandfather passed away
in 1970, about the earliest

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.590
I can come up here and five years later

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.240
I got some insurance
and had cancer surgery,

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.086
and I'm still sitting right here.

00:04:05.086 --> 00:04:06.493
- [Mark] What kind of cancer?

00:04:06.493 --> 00:04:07.843
- Female.

00:04:07.843 --> 00:04:08.764
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:04:08.764 --> 00:04:10.410
- And they closed my restaurant down,

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.856
I couldn't never don't understand that.

00:04:12.856 --> 00:04:14.190
- [Mark] You're the healthiest 87 year old

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:15.400
I think I've ever seen.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.677
- Well, far as I know there's
nothing wrong with me.

00:04:17.677 --> 00:04:20.667
Every bit of my joints is
as limber as they can be,

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:23.570
but I got six joints of backbone,

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.847
now a lot of people may hear this,

00:04:25.847 --> 00:04:30.847
busted up in a wreck, and
that was about in '55 or '56,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.920
and out here at the miner's hospital

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.220
the lady and man was
doctors from over seas

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.063
and they put me on a jello diet.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.310
Each night, now you got a
microwave to heat your water in,

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.270
just set a cup in there, put
a teaspoon full of jello.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.520
I like lemon and lime, and
now you can see how limber,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.310
and I get that guitar
and I can play anything.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.030
My legs is as limber as a 15 year old.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.262
- [Mark] Yeah, you move
like your 30 years old.

00:05:00.262 --> 00:05:04.600
- And that hint of jello
keeps my gelatin in my bones

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.820
and everything built in
my bones, good and sturdy.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.050
It does that the jello
does, it's supposed to.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.070
- [Mark] And your eyesight
is good too, right?

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:11.903
- Huh?

00:05:11.903 --> 00:05:12.736
- [Mark] Your eyesight is good too?

00:05:12.736 --> 00:05:13.569
- Oh yeah!

00:05:13.569 --> 00:05:14.402
- [Mark] And your hearing?

00:05:14.402 --> 00:05:16.710
- Yeah. Nothing wrong no wheres I know of.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:17.673
- [Mark] You don't wear glasses?

00:05:17.673 --> 00:05:18.690
- No.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:19.523
- [Mark] To read?

00:05:19.523 --> 00:05:20.356
- No.

00:05:20.356 --> 00:05:21.189
- [Mark] To drive?

00:05:21.189 --> 00:05:22.022
- Nope.

00:05:22.022 --> 00:05:22.855
- [Mark] You still drive?

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:26.500
- Yup. Drive every day going
here and there or something.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:27.470
- [Mark] You still--

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.440
- They say the Bible says
long as you got work to do,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.560
he'll keep you here,

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.043
so it might be me running the
road in a truck. (laughing)

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.450
- [Mark] You're a
religious? You go to church?

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.324
- Not since my husband died.
I just stay at the house.

00:05:45.324 --> 00:05:47.420
- [Mark] But you would consider yourself

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.200
a spiritual religious person?

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.343
- Oh, yes.

00:05:51.335 --> 00:05:53.290
- [Mark] That's a big thing
here in Appalachia, isn't it?

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.840
- Yeah, usually every
night before I go to bed,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.003
I play Amazing Grace
on my guitar and sang,

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.650
and that tides me over, and my jello.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:06.483
- [Mark] How much schooling did you get?

00:06:06.483 --> 00:06:07.697
How far do you go in school?

00:06:07.697 --> 00:06:12.340
- Ah, I got a high school
education at Berea.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:13.840
We worked our way down there.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.070
Peeled taters in the morning,
wash dishes in the night.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.880
That paid your board and place to stay.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.030
- [Mark] And have you traveled
around the U.S. or the world?

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.030
- I've drug trailers out of
Aurora, and from everywhere

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.380
I reckon in the country.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.990
You buy 'em off, where they
come in with flood disasters.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:39.990
You buy them trailers for
$50-300. Drag in all you want.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.458
- [Mark] No, but have you traveled?

00:06:41.458 --> 00:06:42.291
- Yeah.

00:06:42.291 --> 00:06:43.124
- [Mark] Where have you been?

00:06:43.124 --> 00:06:43.957
- Traveled pulling the trailers,

00:06:43.957 --> 00:06:46.500
and Aurora is about the
furthest way I've been.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.640
Over the ocean, like, there.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:49.473
- [Mark] Aurora?

00:06:49.473 --> 00:06:50.770
- In Florida.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.431
I think it's in Florida.
It's called Aurora.

00:06:53.431 --> 00:06:54.264
- [Mark] Okay.

00:06:54.264 --> 00:06:55.855
- And we come through
Tennessee with 'em here,

00:06:55.855 --> 00:06:58.910
and when we get back, like
you see this mountain.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:00.583
Oh boy, I's a gettin' home.

00:07:00.583 --> 00:07:02.163
I didn't like that level country.

00:07:02.163 --> 00:07:04.020
- [Mark] What is it about Appalachia

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.830
that makes it difficult
for people to leave?

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.310
Because it's a difficult life.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:11.040
- Well we've got, I
think, perfect air here

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.247
to keep you in good health,

00:07:13.247 --> 00:07:15.230
and I don't know people's mind

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:17.613
and everything just stays better.

00:07:18.663 --> 00:07:19.799
- [Mark] I think you're tuned into nature

00:07:19.799 --> 00:07:21.117
and you're living the way--

00:07:21.117 --> 00:07:25.446
- Well, and now, me raising
all my food about it and hogs

00:07:25.446 --> 00:07:27.610
and stuff like that. The meat.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:32.593
And my kids, Roy is 70
year old. My oldest son.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.293
And the baby is 64 lives right
down there, right below here.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:42.140
Roy lives right down there

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:44.703
in that house you first
passed by, the green house.

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.210
- [Mark] So this way of living, yeah,

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:54.010
it's very difficult here
for people to find jobs.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:55.760
- Well, I don't know,

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.420
seems to me like they get paid good wages,

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.947
but right now with people
the mines are going out.

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:02.270
- [Mark] That's what I'm
saying, the coal mines--

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.690
- They just took a coal
out, they go over there,

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:08.280
back of one of my granny Martha Medca

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.925
patented 150 acres over
there back way in the 1800s.

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:15.310
Here down behind it, they set in,

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.720
and they went straight
down where there's coal

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.720
in the back of mine in
the bottom seam was nine

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.560
seams of coal, ten foot high,
that they took and cut out.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.140
And that's the reason
there's no work here,

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.491
they just went to top the mountains

00:08:30.491 --> 00:08:33.020
and just cut the coal out down flat.

00:08:34.984 --> 00:08:38.950
In my opinion now, you know
if they took it all out

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.070
ain't nothing to work.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.250
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:46.250
- And they went down in Gracie
and went under the mines

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.143
and they went through White Oak,

00:08:48.143 --> 00:08:49.700
they went through Wolf Creek,

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.050
and they come out way on
Kuching, busted out the mines.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:54.820
They went all the way
through under the mountains

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.580
and took the coal out. Nobody knowed it.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.610
Nobody got no money. Nobody got nothing.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.480
They took everybody's coal. That's a fact.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.010
I know that because I've
seen where they come out at

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.649
and where they went in at.

00:09:06.649 --> 00:09:10.280
They went plum like right
here, you see them mountains

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:11.113
through here?

00:09:11.113 --> 00:09:13.500
They went down and they was
down at the creek level,

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:14.743
and see now you go over there,

00:09:14.743 --> 00:09:16.940
there's no water in the creeks.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:20.133
I was baptized where
my grandfather drowned,

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.903
and what I call it was a splash down,

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:27.760
and I was over there the
other day and drove by

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.160
and you know what? There ain't
no water over there no more.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.910
There ain't enough to get
out there wet your feet,

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:37.385
in the whole creek. It's awful.

00:09:37.385 --> 00:09:39.926
And I think it's the mining underneath us

00:09:39.926 --> 00:09:43.270
the water's just sinking
Des Pennington told me that

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.180
when I was a kid in White Oak.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.450
- [Mark] Yeah, that would
make sense your digging there.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.530
- He said there'll be a
day when you won't even

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:52.780
see no water washing down this branch.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:54.696
- [Mark] What do you make of the future

00:09:54.696 --> 00:09:57.710
of this part of the world,
part of this country?

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:59.440
- It's coming back to like we growed up.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.683
You're gonna have to
get a mattock and a hoe

00:10:01.683 --> 00:10:03.130
and get out and go to farming
and raising what you eat,

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:04.530
and get your milk cow back,

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.742
and your butter, and hogs, and chickens.

00:10:06.742 --> 00:10:08.210
Our families all got it.

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:11.890
We got 212 acres below
this road down here,

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:15.247
and we've got all them animals down there.

00:10:15.247 --> 00:10:19.430
And then they've got four
horses, so we got wagons,

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:20.696
you can hook them wagons up

00:10:20.696 --> 00:10:22.779
and ride them anywhere you wanna go,

00:10:22.779 --> 00:10:25.750
and all the little tiny stuff

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:28.340
is stored down there from the family.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:29.210
- [Mark] It's a difficult life,

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:32.370
but it seems to make Appalachians
a little more resilient

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:34.300
and compassion for each other.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.487
They were very kind and
helpful for their neighbors.

00:10:36.487 --> 00:10:39.443
- Well as far as I know,
everybody is with me.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:43.087
Everybody treats me like I'm
their sister or their mother,

00:10:43.087 --> 00:10:45.148
and I've rented to so many.

00:10:45.148 --> 00:10:47.714
The kids like him have growed up

00:10:47.714 --> 00:10:50.114
and I don't know them no
more, but they know me.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:56.833
There's too many, and they're so sweet.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.513
I just love them little children.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.093
- [Mark] What was the
best time of your life?

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:07.157
- Well I just wouldn't
know it's all been good.

00:11:07.157 --> 00:11:07.990
- [Mark] That's a great answer.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.317
- And it's still been
good out hittin' the road.

00:11:10.317 --> 00:11:12.830
I have to get up at five
o'clock to get ready to go

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:16.420
and get things done and get
back about seven. (laughing)

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:17.560
- [Mark] Do you feel like
you've lived the life

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:18.630
you wanted to?

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.120
- I live it just like I want to.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.573
If you don't, you've
wasted the whole thing.

00:11:23.728 --> 00:11:26.705
If you sit down and do what
somebody else says for you to do

00:11:26.705 --> 00:11:29.433
that ain't your way of
living, or it ain't mine.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:32.883
I've never let nobody get in my way.

00:11:32.883 --> 00:11:37.533
They get in my way, I'll go
on around 'em. On I'll go.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.900
- [Mark] And your kids are
doing what kind of things?

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:44.793
- Well, they've all retired from jobs.

00:11:46.236 --> 00:11:47.800
- [Mark] But what kind
of work did they do?

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:52.800
- Roy was in social
services, my oldest son.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:56.130
He went to college and he
went in social services.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:59.340
I'm gonna get him started
on his cousin in here,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:04.340
and so Vern, she worked at the court house

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:07.500
in the clerks office till she retired.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:12.160
She passed away three
months ago. She was 66.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.373
She smoked cigarettes and
got cancer of the lungs.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:19.948
Couldn't get rid of 'em at all.

00:12:19.948 --> 00:12:22.760
And my baby one, he got hit in the head

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.973
with the limb of a tree
when he was probably 18.

00:12:27.259 --> 00:12:31.063
I just got three. I lost
two babies at birth.

00:12:35.222 --> 00:12:38.003
And so we've had a fine family.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:42.700
Now we got five, me and
Henry's got five generations

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:46.180
of children, which I'm more
than two probably this week,

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.296
go to the courthouse and get this put in.

00:12:48.296 --> 00:12:52.670
The five generations, it
can't be sold or mortgaged

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:55.363
right here we're on. This park property.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.190
This old Texico park,

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.980
I come here and walked across the hill,

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.750
and went over my grandmother
lived right down here

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:07.990
in June the 15th of '52.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:09.843
Me and Roy, he was two year old,

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:12.470
and we walked across this little hill.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:13.560
Got out right here at the road,

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.540
my grandpa had a store and we walked over

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.270
and come on over here to granny's house,

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:18.693
right down on the hill here,

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:22.630
and Henry went to work at Jellico Grocery,

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.470
which has stayed there about 30 years,

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:27.480
and the mines had shut down

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.180
when we come here at Blue Diamond,

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:32.514
so you had to go where there's work.

00:13:32.514 --> 00:13:34.597
- [Mark] Yeah. Have
you been to big cities?

00:13:34.597 --> 00:13:37.470
- Yeah, I've been, I guess,
in every city they are.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.240
- [Mark] What do you
think of the big city?

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.000
- Well, I don't care for it.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.050
I'd rather be back here in the mountains.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:50.376
It's just too jammed up and you know,

00:13:50.376 --> 00:13:52.510
I like it where it's
like sure you can look,

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:55.110
me I can look a top that
mountain and we are not on it.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:56.380
Next to it.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:57.213
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:13:57.213 --> 00:13:58.210
- And I like that.

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:00.400
- [Mark] You don't have any neighbors.

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:02.340
- Well, I got all these renters.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.750
- [Mark] Yeah, but I
can't see another house

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:05.700
in any direction.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:06.970
- Yeah, not right here.

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.610
I don't allow that because my grandpa.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:12.860
My dad bought out the
heirs and left this to me.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:14.973
It was his part of the estate for me,

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:19.800
and this is the most important
piece of property I've got,

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:22.203
is this old Texico park property.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:30.270
And you oughta take a
picture of that big tree

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:32.920
where the Indians supposed to
have supposedly climb up there

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.530
and watch for people coming in on 'em.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:37.616
They had terms of watching.

00:14:37.616 --> 00:14:39.615
- [Mark] And they'd climb up
there to get a better view?

00:14:39.615 --> 00:14:41.130
- They climb up there
and see they can see 'em

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.829
coming up the mountains on 'em.

00:14:42.829 --> 00:14:44.490
- [Mark] What kind of Indians were here?

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:45.557
- Cherokees.

00:14:45.557 --> 00:14:48.110
- [Mark] Cherokee. Do you have a Cherokee?

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:48.943
- Yes.

00:14:48.943 --> 00:14:49.776
- [Mark] You're part Cherokee?

00:14:49.776 --> 00:14:50.609
- Yeah.

00:14:50.609 --> 00:14:52.150
- [Mark] Somewhere in your--

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:53.950
- Well, I'd say probably I was

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:57.164
a fourth or fifth generation down.

00:14:57.164 --> 00:14:59.650
Maybe not that much. I don't guess.

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:02.095
I probably was second or third generation.

00:15:02.095 --> 00:15:03.581
- [Mark] And tell me
about growing up here.

00:15:03.581 --> 00:15:07.096
So you got vehicles at
what age? Do you remember?

00:15:07.096 --> 00:15:10.014
- Oh I have gotten every vehicle but four

00:15:10.014 --> 00:15:13.260
that I bought since I
went to drive in '52.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:15.080
- [Mark] '52 is when
vehicle kinda came to your--

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:17.230
- Yeah, oh, that's older than that.

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:19.237
They're down on the hill,
all these down through here,

00:15:19.237 --> 00:15:20.880
then down at my house.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.410
- [Mark] No, but you went how
many years before the vehicle

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:25.064
really came into this part of the country?

00:15:25.064 --> 00:15:28.713
- Well, I was down at
the foot of the mountain.

00:15:28.713 --> 00:15:32.994
I just really don't know.
In '52, I was born in '34,

00:15:32.994 --> 00:15:37.154
so I didn't go to drivin'.
'34, '44, '54, '52.

00:15:37.154 --> 00:15:38.904
18 I went to driving.

00:15:40.224 --> 00:15:41.835
- [Mark] Okay, so automobile came to--

00:15:41.835 --> 00:15:46.835
- Yeah I've got, I've sold
two since then. Since '52.

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:50.137
Two vehicles I let go,
I trade them in on too,

00:15:50.137 --> 00:15:52.220
and after that I just kept them

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:54.427
and bought me something else.

00:15:54.427 --> 00:15:55.977
- [Mark] And you had outhouses?

00:15:55.977 --> 00:15:57.920
I think you still have an
outhouse now, don't you?

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.220
- I guess they are out houses.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:01.800
- [Mark] No, didn't I see
one next to your house?

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:06.290
- For people to use, no
them ain't outhouses.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.844
They're stuffed full
of old time-y antiques.

00:16:08.844 --> 00:16:11.070
- [Mark] Oh, okay. It
looked like an outhouse.

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.110
- They've got all kinds
of antiques in them.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:14.630
- [Mark] But that's
probably how you grew up.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.817
With outhouses and stuff like that?

00:16:16.817 --> 00:16:19.910
- Yeah, I grew up that
way and up here we had 'em

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:23.013
pretty well till they put
the water down the mountain,

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.104
and that's not been probably,
what, 12-15 year ago

00:16:27.104 --> 00:16:28.320
if it's been that long.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.370
- [Mark] Yeah, there's still people living

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:32.030
with no electricity, no
running water, today.

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:33.920
- Yeah, I'd say they are.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:35.240
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.380
- And a lot of people
use generators anymore.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:39.350
- [Mark] Right.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:40.833
- Like if I rent a place for them

00:16:40.833 --> 00:16:43.210
just to pull in a camper and live,

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:45.937
they have generators just to hook 'em up.

00:16:45.937 --> 00:16:47.660
Back on Dingo

00:16:50.724 --> 00:16:52.360
- [Mark] Do you have
any regrets in you life?

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.830
- Any regrets? No I
don't fool with nothing

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:57.309
that would 'cause me a regret.

00:16:57.309 --> 00:16:58.340
Only thing, you know,

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:01.263
we always hate you've lost a
baby or something like that,

00:17:01.263 --> 00:17:05.643
that you didn't get to be with.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:10.897
Henry lived to be 85, so
you can't regret that,

00:17:10.897 --> 00:17:14.353
and my dad was, I think he was about 96,

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:18.200
and then I kept my uncle
and took care of him

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:21.403
and I'm not for sure what his
age was when he passed away.

00:17:22.565 --> 00:17:25.603
I've kept the family and took
care of them as they passed.

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:29.380
- [Mark] What's the most
important lesson you've learned

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:30.533
in your 87 years?

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.790
- To be saving with my money! (laughing)

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:39.790
The youngins is gonna need
it. The children. (laughing)

00:17:40.159 --> 00:17:42.629
- [Mark] And your husband
did what kind of work?

00:17:42.629 --> 00:17:45.307
- He worked in the mines first,

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:49.100
and then one thing I'd like to tell you,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:53.010
Mr. Corman, him, James Corman,

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:58.010
they were masons of the Nolan
Lodge up at Pine Mountain.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:01.603
And I'm getting ready
to send to the lawyers,

00:18:01.603 --> 00:18:04.533
some pictures of Mr. Corman, right now,

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:08.733
and I think thems really important things.

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:13.610
My husband joined the Nolan
Lodge at 22 years old,

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.379
we was a dating and he
couldn't hardly come

00:18:16.379 --> 00:18:18.973
on Saturday night 'cause
he had to go up there,

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:23.690
and we dated 27 months for we got married,

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:27.260
'cause I was in Berea
school most of the time.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:29.950
And I come in that fall, that summer,

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:31.420
and I didn't go back at fall.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:32.957
We just got married, he
had me a house built,

00:18:32.957 --> 00:18:36.180
and it furnished, so I just went home

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:39.193
and I've had a home ever since. (laughing)

00:18:40.519 --> 00:18:42.440
- [Mark] But you've lived a
great life, in your opinion?

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.077
- I don't think that anybody's
lived the life I have,

00:18:45.077 --> 00:18:47.570
and it's all been great, and I go to town,

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:50.230
everybody hollers, Ruby Baker, Ruby Baker.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:51.830
Comes running to me.

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:54.600
Them kids have growed up and
I don't know who they are,

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:57.740
but I don't tell them
that. I don't say anything.

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.370
I just meet them, and
maybe hug their neck.

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:01.530
- [Mark] But it's not your memory,

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:02.378
it's just that there are so many.

00:19:02.378 --> 00:19:04.128
- They so many, yeah.

00:19:05.277 --> 00:19:07.520
- [Mark] But your memory is
sharp? Your health is great?

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:12.520
- Yeah, I've been in business,
since I guess I was what,

00:19:15.323 --> 00:19:18.580
maybe 22 when I put up the rest
at the foot of the mountain.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:19.413
- [Mark] Do you think staying busy,

00:19:19.413 --> 00:19:21.130
staying working, active, is the key to--

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:22.884
- Oh, if you sit down, you know,

00:19:22.884 --> 00:19:24.690
you're going to get big
and fat, and you can't get.

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:27.050
I've got renters, can't get
out of their wheelchair.

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:29.270
If they get down on the floor.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:30.440
If anybody's listening,

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:33.680
the best thing to do is
lay flat on the floor,

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:35.900
or put you down a pad or a mattress.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:39.080
You twist this leg over.
Then you twist that leg over.

00:19:39.080 --> 00:19:41.798
Then you roll this back
over and that back over,

00:19:41.798 --> 00:19:44.498
and you can twist every bit
of that weight off of you.

00:19:45.345 --> 00:19:46.940
See, look at me.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:48.547
- [Mark] You look perfect.

00:19:48.547 --> 00:19:51.198
- And I'm just as limber as a dishrag.

00:19:51.198 --> 00:19:53.698
(Mark laughs)

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:56.653
- [Mark] Cute as can be.

00:19:57.610 --> 00:19:59.040
Darlin', thank you so
much for talking with me

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:00.190
and sharing your story.

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:02.813
You're amazing.

00:20:02.813 --> 00:20:04.090
- It's been wonderful meeting you.

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:05.710
- [Mark] Yeah, likewise. I wish you--

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:08.330
- Maybe somebody will
see something in this

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.803
will help them, and they'll
be here when they 87,

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:16.390
or will be 87 here what? 4-5 more months.

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:17.223
- [Mark] I'll bet you you're gonna make it

00:20:17.223 --> 00:20:18.570
well past a hundred.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:21.850
- Well, my family usually
lives to be 104 or five.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:24.700
- [Mark] Yeah, I don't see
you slowing down anytime soon.

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.890
- Not without something
much to come up, you know,

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:31.740
it could get in a wreck or
anything can happen every day,

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:35.670
but you don't worry about
that. You just keep a trucking.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.667
But now one thing is we
pick a lot of huckleberries.

00:20:38.667 --> 00:20:40.850
See all over these little ridges.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:43.842
We pick our blackberries, put them away.

00:20:43.842 --> 00:20:47.440
I've got peaches, apples put away.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.240
I got pumpkins down there in
the house, new this winter,

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:55.163
and I think a lot of good
healthy food is good.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:57.990
But everybody don't
live where they can have

00:20:57.990 --> 00:20:59.592
hogs and stuff like us.

00:20:59.592 --> 00:21:01.707
- [Mark] No, they don't.

00:21:01.707 --> 00:21:04.090
- That's the reason you
gotta keep this land,

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:06.880
and right now get it in five generations.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:08.183
I've got five generations, you can put it,

00:21:08.183 --> 00:21:10.013
that they can't sell it that long,

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.435
or mortgage it, and I
don't want either one.

00:21:14.435 --> 00:21:16.842
- [Mark] You wanna play a
little Amazing Grace for me?

00:21:16.842 --> 00:21:17.967
- [Ruby] If you want me to?

00:21:17.967 --> 00:21:18.875
- [Mark] Here.

00:21:18.875 --> 00:21:20.857
(guitar strums)

00:21:20.857 --> 00:21:23.096
- Now when I get ready to
go to bed at the night,

00:21:23.096 --> 00:21:25.179
that's usually what I do.

00:21:26.216 --> 00:21:29.917
♪ Amazing Grace ♪

00:21:29.917 --> 00:21:33.107
♪ How sweet the sound ♪

00:21:33.107 --> 00:21:38.107
♪ That saved a wretch like me ♪

00:21:38.856 --> 00:21:41.654
♪ I once was lost ♪

00:21:41.654 --> 00:21:44.396
♪ But now I'm found ♪

00:21:44.396 --> 00:21:45.764
♪ I was blind ♪

00:21:45.764 --> 00:21:49.877
♪ But now I see ♪

00:21:49.877 --> 00:21:50.710
♪ When you been there ♪

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:53.841
♪ Ten thousand years ♪

00:21:53.841 --> 00:21:58.841
♪ Bright shining as the sun ♪

00:22:00.664 --> 00:22:02.776
♪ We've no less days ♪

00:22:02.776 --> 00:22:05.486
♪ To sing God's praise ♪

00:22:05.486 --> 00:22:10.486
♪ Than when we'd first begun ♪

00:22:10.975 --> 00:22:13.655
♪ When I've got my mind made up ♪

00:22:13.655 --> 00:22:16.496
♪ And my heart is right ♪

00:22:16.496 --> 00:22:21.009
♪ And I'm going with Jesus all the way ♪

00:22:21.009 --> 00:22:23.569
♪ Got my mind made up ♪

00:22:23.569 --> 00:22:26.264
♪ And my heart is right ♪

00:22:26.264 --> 00:22:31.264
♪ And I'm going with Jesus all the way ♪

00:22:31.327 --> 00:22:36.327
♪ Lord I feel that Holy
Spirit climbing down ♪

00:22:36.586 --> 00:22:41.276
♪ Lord I feel that holy
spirit climbing down ♪

00:22:41.276 --> 00:22:44.931
♪ It's the power of our nation ♪

00:22:44.931 --> 00:22:46.413
♪ We can read the Revelations ♪

00:22:46.413 --> 00:22:51.413
♪ Lord I feel the Holy
spirit climbing down ♪

00:22:55.344 --> 00:22:56.427
- How's that?

00:22:56.427 --> 00:22:57.757
- [Mark] Beautiful.

00:22:57.757 --> 00:22:59.270
- That's all I have to have,

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.610
just play that if anything's bothering me,

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:04.593
Lord I just feel that good
spirit, maybe it's crazy,

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:09.393
but I just feel that
and I can just march on.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:13.563
I feel like I can just
march so far at that time.

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:16.555
Well I have. (laughing)

00:23:16.555 --> 00:23:19.100
My house caught on fire
and I had to run out of it!

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:21.100
- [Mark] Beautiful,
Ruby. Thank you so much.

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:23.180
- Oh Lord, now that'll make my day.

00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:24.013
- [Mark] That was beautiful.

00:23:24.013 --> 00:23:25.892
- I can sang it and I can really get on,

00:23:25.892 --> 00:23:27.874
and I think right there is the reason

00:23:27.874 --> 00:23:30.624
that I don't get sick or nothing.

00:23:32.302 --> 00:23:33.543
- [Mark] Yeah, I buy that.

00:23:33.543 --> 00:23:36.010
- If everybody can live like that,

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:37.783
wouldn't the world be different?

00:23:37.783 --> 00:23:41.653
All this medicine could
be cut out. Everything.

00:23:41.653 --> 00:23:43.430
That'd be wonderful.

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:44.263
- [Mark] Ruby, you are spectacular.

00:23:44.263 --> 00:23:46.433
- [Ruby] I'm just an old time woman.

00:23:46.433 --> 00:23:47.663
(Mark laughs)

00:23:47.663 --> 00:23:49.751
Been here many, many years.

00:23:49.751 --> 00:23:51.364
